class.description:
A [VirtualTexture2D] is a texture far larger than what fits in video memory - 16384×16384 texels or more - of which only the parts the camera actually sees, at the resolution it sees them at, are ever loaded. It is cut into pages of [constant RenderingServer.VIRTUAL_TEXTURE_PAGE_SIZE]×[constant RenderingServer.VIRTUAL_TEXTURE_PAGE_SIZE] texels at every mipmap. While rendering, the GPU writes down which pages it sampled; those not in video memory yet are read from disk and decoded on a thread of their own and copied into a shared cache, a few every frame, while the closest coarser page already there stands in for them. Pages no longer seen are evicted to make room for new ones. A texture of any size therefore costs the same fixed cache (see [member ProjectSettings.rendering/virtual_texturing/streamed_cache_size]) and one small page table.
A texture becomes virtual by converting it in the FileSystem dock: right-click an image and choose [b]Convert to Virtual Texture[/b] (it is reimported with [ResourceImporterVirtualTexture]; [b]Convert to Regular Texture[/b] turns it back). Nothing else needs to change: every scene, material and script referencing the file keeps referencing it.
[StandardMaterial3D] and [ORMMaterial3D] stream every texture assigned to them that is virtual (except with triplanar mapping). A [ShaderMaterial] streams the ones it samples through a uniform with the [code]hint_virtual_texture[/code] hint:
[codeblock lang=text]
shader_type spatial;

uniform sampler2D albedo_texture : source_color, hint_virtual_texture;

void fragment() {
	ALBEDO = texture(albedo_texture, UV).rgb;
}
[/codeblock]
Such a uniform can be sampled with [code]texture()[/code], [code]textureLod()[/code] and [code]textureGrad()[/code], and measured with [code]textureSize()[/code]. Anywhere else - a uniform without the hint, a 2D node, the Compatibility renderer, or a GPU that cannot do virtual texturing (see [method RenderingServer.is_virtual_texturing_supported]) - the texture is a small copy of itself, at most 256 texels a side (see [method get_fallback_image]).
[b]Note:[/b] Whatever a material's filter setting, virtual textures are always sampled with linear filtering between linearly interpolated mipmaps, and with anisotropic filtering as set by [member ProjectSettings.rendering/textures/default_filters/anisotropic_filtering_level], up to 4×.
